{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unmelted-grieving-blowfish","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"# S4 — Bound `/var/tmp` growth and alarm on tmp saturation\n\n## Plan\nAdd an enforced ceiling for `/var/tmp`, not just ad-hoc cleanup. Use systemd-tmpfiles for age cleanup and choose a real size-enforcing mechanism compatible with the host filesystem. If `/var/tmp` remains on root/XFS and tmpfiles cannot enforce a byte cap by itself, implement a bounded host-level cleanup service/timer or mount/quota arrangement that makes the size cap true. Do not write probe or gate evidence to `/var/gerrit`; that sibling concern is tracked by `scowlful-bovine-dragonfly`.\n\nExtend observability to report `/var/tmp` usage relative to its configured cap and add its own CloudWatch alarm. Document what may be safely removed and how the age/size policy interacts with long-running processes.\n\n## Grounding\n- `/var/tmp` was identified as a root accumulator in the incident.\n- `infra/scripts/observability.sh:72-90` currently reports aggregate filesystems only; no per-generator `/var/tmp` metric exists.\n- systemd-tmpfiles supports age-based cleanup (`d`/`e` entries) and btrfs qgroup directory helpers, but on non-btrfs filesystems a separate size-enforcement mechanism may be required.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `/var/tmp` has documented age cleanup and an actual size ceiling that is enforced on the Gerrit host.\n- [ ] The implementation states why the chosen size mechanism works on AL2023/root filesystem assumptions.\n- [ ] Observability publishes `/var/tmp` usage/cap metrics and Terraform adds a dedicated alarm wired to SNS.\n- [ ] Runbook documents safe remediation and forbids using `/var/gerrit` as generic investigation scratch.\n- [ ] Targeted tests cover tmpfiles/timer/config generation, metric emission, and alarm wiring.\n- [ ] Portability criterion: uses Linux host-level facilities and local tests; no GitHub-only CI signal is introduced.\n\n## Design ambiguity to resolve before implementation\nTmpfiles alone is primarily age cleanup on XFS; the implementation must choose the concrete byte-cap mechanism (separate mounted filesystem, quota-capable path, or bounded cleanup timer).","id":"2ba3-bf77-1303-4b2d","parent_id":"6202-e1c7-c57f-4897","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":["infra","gerrit","disk-ceiling","tmpfiles"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"S4 — Bound /var/tmp growth and alarm on tmp saturation"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788375027122404696,"uuid":"38033a20-a39a-47db-bb14-17dc8a364777"}